(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-hf-digoxin-quiz"]={"title":"Digoxin Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which intracellular ion rises first when digoxin blocks the sodium-potassium pump, and then raises intracellular calcium?","choices":["Potassium","Chloride","Sodium","Magnesium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pump inhibition traps sodium in the cell, and the sodium-calcium exchanger then raises intracellular calcium, so the fibers shorten with more force. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which serum potassium goal protects a patient taking digoxin together with a thiazide diuretic?","choices":["Below 3.5 milliequivalents per liter","Above 4.0 milliequivalents per liter","Between 3.0 and 3.5 milliequivalents per liter","Any value if magnesium is normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides lower potassium and raise digitalis toxicity, so potassium should be kept above 4.0 milliequivalents per liter. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which conduction disorder contraindicates digoxin?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation with heart failure","Normal sinus rhythm","Advanced atrioventricular block","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin slows nodal conduction, so advanced atrioventricular block is a contraindication. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which diuretic-related abnormality raises the risk of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Hyperuricemia","Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Hypophosphatemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia, especially from loop or thiazide diuretics, raises digitalis toxicity and arrhythmia risk, together with hypomagnesemia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which heart rate disorder contraindicates digoxin?","choices":["Severe bradycardia or sick sinus syndrome","Atrial fibrillation with a rapid rate","Mild sinus tachycardia","Normal sinus rhythm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin slows the nodes further, so severe bradycardia and sick sinus syndrome are contraindications. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which enzyme does digoxin inhibit?","choices":["Phosphodiesterase 3","Sodium-potassium ATPase","Neprilysin","Carbonic anhydrase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump, which is the starting point of both its inotropic and its neurohormonal actions. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which gastrointestinal symptoms are early signs of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Constipation and bloating","Anorexia and nausea","Heartburn and belching","Abdominal cramps with bloody stool"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anorexia and nausea are the gastrointestinal signs listed for digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which properties of digoxin do its attached sugar molecules influence?","choices":["Absorption, half-life and metabolism","Activation of beta-1 receptors","Selectivity for atrial over ventricular cells","Affinity for digoxin immune Fab fragments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sugar molecules shape how digoxin is absorbed, how long it lasts and how it is metabolized; activity comes from the lactone ring and steroid nucleus. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug rapidly reverses life-threatening digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Atropine","Digoxin immune Fab","Protamine sulfate","Naloxone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin immune Fab is an antibody that binds digoxin and rapidly reverses toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram change accompanies digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Lengthened PR interval","Shortened PR interval","Lengthened QT interval","Elevated ST segment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nodal slowing lengthens the PR interval, shortens the QT interval and depresses the ST segment. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"From which animal is digoxin immune Fab produced?","choices":["Rabbits","Horses","Goats","Sheep"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Healthy sheep are immunized with digoxin coupled to human serum albumin, and their antibody is the source of the antidote. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which clinical benefit does digoxin provide in heart failure?","choices":["Fewer hospitalizations","Longer survival","Slower disease progression","Reversal of the underlying cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin improves symptoms, exercise tolerance and quality of life and lowers the number of hospitalizations. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which autonomic change is part of the newer, neurohormonal action of digoxin?","choices":["Higher sympathetic and lower parasympathetic activity","Higher sympathetic and higher parasympathetic activity","Lower sympathetic and lower parasympathetic activity","Lower sympathetic and higher parasympathetic activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin lowers sympathetic and raises parasympathetic nervous system activity, which slows the heart and resensitizes the baroreflex. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which symptom of digoxin toxicity arises from the central nervous system?","choices":["Hearing loss","Gynecomastia","Dry cough","Abnormal dreams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Delirium, fatigue, confusion, dizziness and abnormal dreams are the central symptoms of digoxin toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which drug family does digoxin belong to?","choices":["Phosphodiesterase 3 inhibitor","Beta-1 receptor agonist","Digitalis glycoside","Hyperpolarization-activated channel blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin is a digitalis glycoside: a lactone ring and steroid nucleus with attached sugars, acting on the sodium-potassium pump of cardiac cells. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which type of ventricular dysfunction in symptomatic heart failure is digoxin considered for?","choices":["Diastolic dysfunction","Systolic dysfunction","Right atrial enlargement","Isolated pericardial effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin is considered for symptomatic heart failure with systolic dysfunction, where contractility is reduced. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which heart rate change is a classic cardiac sign of digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","A fixed rapid regular rate","Bradycardia","Heart rate with no change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nodal slowing makes bradycardia the classically listed cardiac sign, although premature ventricular beats are also very common and digoxin can provoke almost any arrhythmia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which rhythm disorder alongside heart failure makes digoxin useful for rate control?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Sick sinus syndrome","Advanced atrioventricular block","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin's rate-slowing properties make it useful for rate control in atrial fibrillation together with heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why is the digoxin concentration kept in a target range rather than pushed higher?","choices":["Higher concentrations may give worse outcomes","Higher concentrations remove the survival gap","Lower concentrations cause toxicity first","Higher concentrations lose inotropic effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Higher concentrations in heart failure may be associated with worse outcomes, and they raise toxicity risk without added benefit. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"}]};
